**Read:** [Air-cleaning systems — DOE-HDBK-1169-2022](../sources/Air-Cleaning-Handbook-DOE.lingua#r_09QPEZ76E9W6HS7R55X2K8TB3X), sections 4.1.5.3 and 4.1.9 on installed systems; revisit [Recirculation, filtration and bypass](../transport/Recirculation-filtration-and-bypass.lingua#r_2PYGBXM8TFGPQ1WGHP4CSRDFAA).

Suppose fraction b of total flow bypasses the medium and has the same upstream concentration, while the remaining fraction crosses medium with penetration Pm. Perfect downstream mixing gives Psystem=b+(1−b)Pm. The fractions describe airflow, not the fraction of visible housing area occupied by a gap.

With hypothetical b=0.001 and Pm=0.0001, Psystem=0.0010999. The installed penetration is nearly eleven times the medium penetration. Inverting gives b=(Psystem−Pm)/(1−Pm), but only under the model's equal upstream concentration, representative sampling and mixing assumptions.

For linear parallel paths with conductances Gb and Gm, b=Gb/(Gb+Gm). Different flow exponents or changing media resistance make b depend on the operating state. A fixed visible gap does not imply a fixed bypass fraction.

**Work:** Derive the forward and inverse balances, then propagate measurement uncertainty into inferred b. Compare a peripheral leak, a damaged region and a downstream source: one concentration ratio cannot identify all three. Explain what a spatial scan, flow change and upstream mixing check can distinguish, and why installed integrity, medium efficiency and room performance need separate evidence.

